FLOORFORGE_42 — the estimator's perimeter was a constant. A floor's perimeter
is not.

THE DEFECT

`edgingLinearFt` was an independent input in components/JobEstimator.tsx,
initialised to 180 and never touched by anything else. Change the floor area and
it did not move.

  1,200 sqft  ->  180 lin ft
  8,500 sqft  ->  180 lin ft
 20,000 sqft  ->  180 lin ft

The edging line is `edgingLinearFt / edgingLinearFtPerHour`, so on an 8,500 sqft
job the estimator billed 4.5 crew-hours of edging where the geometry says 9.4.
That is roughly five hours a contractor quoted and did not get paid for, in the
one FloorForge product that exists today and that someone might actually put a
price in front of a homeowner with.

It also contradicted the site. public/llms.txt, the published throughput figure
and the entire two-machine argument all rest on ONE relationship:

  "a perimeter grows with the square root of the area while the field grows
   with the area"

Every surface said that. The tool a contractor uses treated the perimeter as a
constant — which is the same class of defect as a wrong number, and worse,
because it is the number that leaves the building on a quote.

THE FIX

One room model for the whole product, rather than a second one here.

  lib/floorPlan.ts
    * `ROOM_ASPECT` (4/3) exported instead of written inline as `(area * 4) / 3`.
      `planFloor` now reads it. Identical arithmetic, one definition — a second
      copy of "4:3" in another file is exactly how the simulator and the live
      console came to disagree about room size in the first place.
    * `rectPerimeter(area)` — perimeter of a ROOM_ASPECT rectangle. UNIT-AGNOSTIC:
      perimeter scales with the square root of area, so the units cancel and the
      imperial estimator can share geometry with the metric machine model
      instead of keeping its own.

  lib/estimator.ts
    * `suggestedEdgingLinearFt(sqft)`, rounded to 5 ft because this is an
      estimate and should not read like a survey.

  components/JobEstimator.tsx
    * The default is derived, not 180.
    * LINKED UNTIL TOUCHED. The perimeter follows the area until the contractor
      types their own, and then never moves again. Always-linked would be worse
      than the frozen 180: a real floor's closets and islands are knowledge only
      they have, and silently overwriting a number they entered is the one thing
      a tool must not do.
    * Opening a saved job marks it touched. A stored perimeter is already the
      contractor's decision, whether they typed it or accepted ours.
    * The field carries a description, wired with `aria-describedby` so a screen
      reader hears the provenance on focus rather than having to hunt for it.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

It does not invent a complexity multiplier. A bare rectangle is a LOWER BOUND —
no closets, no island, no interior partitions, and every one of those adds wall
the edger has to follow. A 1.15x fudge would make the number look more right and
be less honest, and nobody at FloorForge has measured one. So the derived figure
is presented as a minimum, in the field's own description, with what raises it
named: "Closets, islands and interior walls add to it — raise this to match the
floor." The input stays fully editable, as every assumption in this tool is.

  Derived, for reference:
       500 sqft ->    90 lin ft
     1,200 sqft ->   140 lin ft   (was 180)
     2,500 sqft ->   200 lin ft
     8,500 sqft ->   375 lin ft   (was 180)
    20,000 sqft ->   570 lin ft   (was 180)

